# =============================================================================
# Terraform
# =============================================================================

# Provider binaries and module cache — re-created by `terraform init`.
.terraform/

# STATE IS A SECRET.
#
# It records every resource attribute in plaintext, including values a
# configuration marks `sensitive`. For this project that means the SSH public
# key, the full rendered cloud-init user-data, and the network layout. It is also
# machine-specific and merge-hostile.
#
# The fix for "my colleague needs the state" is a remote backend, not a commit.
# See terraform/backend.tf.
*.tfstate
*.tfstate.*
.terraform.tfstate.lock.info

# Real variable values. The .example file IS committed — it is the documentation.
*.tfvars
*.tfvars.json
!*.tfvars.example

# Saved plans contain the same secrets as state.
*.tfplan
plan.out

crash.log
crash.*.log

# NOT ignored, deliberately: .terraform.lock.hcl.
# The lock file pins provider checksums. Committing it is what makes `init`
# reproducible for the next person and stops a silent provider upgrade from
# changing behaviour. Terraform prints a reminder about this on first init.

# =============================================================================
# Ansible
# =============================================================================

# Generated by Terraform on every apply. Names a private key path and changes
# whenever the VM is rebuilt.
#
# No extension on purpose: Ansible's inventory directory scan ignores `.ini`
# (it is in inventory_ignore_extensions), so hosts.ini inside inventory/ would be
# silently skipped. See ansible/ansible.cfg.
inventory/hosts
ansible/inventory/hosts

# YOUR REAL HOSTS. A public repo must never carry the addresses and usernames of
# machines you actually run. Create it with `make vps-inventory`, which copies
# ansible/examples/vps.example into place.
inventory/vps
ansible/inventory/vps

# THE VAULT ITSELF IS NOT COMMITTED.
#
# Ansible Vault uses AES-256, so committing the encrypted file is not reckless —
# and for a private repo it is the conventional choice, since the secret then
# travels with the code that consumes it.
#
# This repository is public, which changes the calculus completely. Published
# ciphertext can be downloaded by anyone, forever, and attacked offline at
# leisure. Its only protection is the passphrase, and "my passphrase is strong"
# is not a control you can audit or rotate. So: the vault stays local, and every
# clone generates its own with `make vault-init`.
#
# The committed vault.yml.example documents the variable names, which is the part
# a reader actually needs.
#
# When a team genuinely needs to share secrets through git, the answer is SOPS +
# age or a KMS — encryption whose key lives outside the repository, not a
# passphrase in someone's head. See docs/PORTING-TO-VPS.md.
inventory/group_vars/all/vault.yml
ansible/inventory/group_vars/all/vault.yml

# The vault password. Never committed under any policy.
.vault-pass
.vault_pass
*.vaultpass
vault-password*

# A decrypted vault is a plaintext secret file.
vault.yml.decrypted
*.decrypted

ansible/collections/
*.retry
.ansible/
ansible/.cache/
fact_cache/

# =============================================================================
# Local
# =============================================================================
.env
*.pem
*.key
!ansible/roles/*/files/*.key.j2

.DS_Store
*.swp
*~
.idea/
.vscode/

# Filled-in runbooks naming real hosts. The generic procedure lives in
# docs/RUNBOOK-ENROL-PUBLIC-VPS.md with placeholders and IS committed; anything under
# docs/local/ carries actual addresses, ports and account names and is not.
docs/local/
